<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taʻu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taʻu is the largest and easternmost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manua-islands">Manuʻa Islands</a> in American Samoa, a volcanic island rising to Lata Mountain, the highest point in the territory. It was the residence of the Tuʻi Manuʻa, the paramount chief of Manuʻa. The anthropologist Margaret Mead did the fieldwork for Coming of Age in Samoa (1928) there, and much of the island is national park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tau-american-samoa.jpg" alt="Taʻu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ta%27u_Island.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Taʻū is the largest inhabited island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manua-islands">Manuʻa Islands</a> and the easternmost volcanic island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoan Islands</a>. In the early 19th century it was sometimes called Opoun. It is the eroded remnant of a hotspot shield volcano with a caldera complex on its south face. Lata Mountain, at 931 metres, is the highest point in American Samoa, and the south coast has sea cliffs rising to 3,000 feet. The island has a land area of 44.31 square kilometres and had 790 people at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p>On its western coast the adjoining villages of Lumā and Siufaga are together called Taʻū village, named capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manua-islands">Manuʻa Islands</a>. The south-eastern half of the island is part of the National Park of American Samoa, which includes the ancient sacred site of Saua, considered the birthplace of the Polynesian people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the three islands of the Manuʻa Group formed one polity, and Taʻū was its main centre of authority, home of the Tui Manuʻa paramount chieftaincy. In 1986 researchers found ceramic-bearing sites in Taʻū village, including a sherd of Polynesian Plain Ware, and test excavations reached deeper layers of the Ancestral Polynesian period.</p><p>On 13 June 1722 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-roggeveen">Jacob Roggeveen</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> sighted the island. The people placed offerings of food along the beach for what they called the Sailing Gods, and several men paddled out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe">outrigger canoes</a> and climbed aboard to trade coconuts for metal nails. After barely two hours Roggeveen sailed west.</p><p>On 11 March 1900 Commander Benjamin Franklin Tilley anchored off Taʻū village and asked the Tui Manuʻa to join the chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> in signing a Deed of Cession to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. At a meeting the next day, Tui Manuʻa Elisala questioned the right of foreign powers to interfere in what he considered a sovereign state. He acknowledged American sovereignty in general terms and accepted its protection, but refused to cede his own lands on Taʻū. The island was where Margaret Mead, then aged 23, carried out the research in the 1920s that she published in 1928 as Coming of Age in Samoa. A hurricane in early 1987 destroyed nearly all the homes on the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Taʻū forms part of the Manuʻa District of American Samoa with Ofu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olosega">Olosega</a>, and is divided into three counties: Faleāsao, Fitiʻuta and Taʻū. The Taʻū unit of the national park covers about 2,025 hectares and is the park's largest section. Since 2016 the island has drawn almost all its electricity from a solar array with battery storage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taʻū” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Manuʻa group, the islands of <b>Taʻu</b>, Ofu and Olosega, lies at the eastern end of the Samoan archipelago.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-manu-a">Kingdom of Manuʻa</a></li></ul></section>
